## The GIL

GIL = Global Interpreter Lock.

In traditional CPython, the GIL prevents multiple threads from executing Python bytecode simultaneously within the same interpreter.

This means:

    Multiple threads
        ↓
    Usually cannot execute CPU-heavy Python code
    simultaneously in one traditional CPython interpreter

However, threads are still very useful for I/O-bound work.

For example:

    Thread 1 → waiting for website
    Thread 2 → waiting for database
    Thread 3 → waiting for file

While one thread waits, another can make progress.

Modern Python also has free-threaded CPython builds that can run without the traditional GIL, so the GIL should not be treated as an absolute property of every Python implementation/build.

