## Final Goal

You should understand:

    - How to measure performance
    - How to profile Python programs
    - How to identify bottlenecks
    - How algorithms affect performance
    - How data structures affect performance
    - How memory usage affects programs
    - Lazy evaluation and generators
    - Caching
    - CPU-bound vs I/O-bound workloads
    - Threads vs multiprocessing vs asyncio
    - What the GIL is
    - The basic CPython execution model
    - Bytecode
    - Reference counting
    - Garbage collection
    - How Python can use compiled C/C++ code
    - When vectorisation or native code is worthwhile

The main principle:

    Measure → Identify the bottleneck → Optimise → Measure again.

You do NOT need to become a CPython internals expert to write fast Python.
The important skill is knowing where performance problems come from and choosing the appropriate solution.